Close the orphaned registration window and fuzz sign_commit (#108, #95) (#154)

* fix(reconciler): gate SPIRE entry-missing/not-deleted drift by event age (#108)

ADR-0018 has the ledger write precede the SPIRE mutation in both
directions (register_agent appends before it creates; retire_agent and
the reaper record before they delete). internal/spire's entry
reconciler (RM-019) compared ledger and SPIRE state with no notion of
age, so a run inside that legitimate window was indistinguishable from
one truly orphaned - and OPS-003 found three runs that crashed inside
register_agent's window, with run_registered on the chain, no SPIRE
entry, and no closing event, that nothing autonomous ever caught.

Add ReconcilerConfig.MinAge (default six minutes, derived from
register_agent's worst-case RPC latency plus IP §6.8's clock-skew
tolerance) and gate DriftEntryMissing/DriftEntryNotDeleted on it, via
the run_registered/closing event's own ts. DriftEntryUnattributed and
DriftEntryDuplicated are unaffected: their read order already makes
them race-free under ADR-0018, and gating them would only delay
detection of a genuinely planted or duplicated identity.

TestSPI009 proves a run inside the window raises nothing; TestSPI010
proves one past it is recorded as ledger_drift_detected exactly once,
with the idempotency key never replayed - the caller that has died for
good, not the one MCP-011/REC-002 already cover. New test IDs SPI-009
and SPI-010, not yet in doc 07 (human to add, per SPI-008's precedent).

* fix(reconciler): extract parseTS to satisfy errcheck (#108)

golangci-lint's errcheck flagged the two sites that discarded
event.ParseTimestamp's error with _. Both already tolerated a bad
timestamp by falling through to the zero value (oldEnough already
treats zero as "cannot judge"), so the fix is a named helper that
makes that tolerance explicit and checked, not a nolint.

* test(failure): fuzz sign_commit in the crash-and-replay campaign (#95)

RM-033 bound sign_commit and MCP-011's own doc comment flagged itself as
out of date: IP §6.6's third clause, "never a second commit", was untested
because until now no tool made one.

signcommitharness_test.go brings up its own Sigstore-capable daemon against
RM-034's requireSigStack, and drives the two named windows from IP §6.5
(commit_intent with no signature, and a signed commit with no
commit_recorded) by holding the wrapper's own requests to Fulcio's
certificate endpoint and to its Rekor search — both exact-path matches on
sigFailProxy that leave gitsign's own traffic (the trust fetch, its own
Rekor upload) untouched, so the held window is real and not raced for.

A lease takeover landing in the commit-recorded-missing window is asserted
to be REFUSED rather than repaired (ADR-0031 decision 6 keeps
internal/signing's Rekor search unexported on purpose), and "never a
second commit" is checked by counting commit objects via git plumbing the
way RM-034's SIG-002/003 do, never by comparing replies.

scripts/test-no-skips.sh's ALLOWED entry for this subtest is removed, per
its own comment; that gate must now pass with sign_commit fuzzed rather
than skipped.

